What I covered

Agentic AI — LLM agents in Bedrock, the deprecation of Bedrock Agents, multi-agent workflows, short and long-term agent memory, Strands Agents, Agent Squad, Amazon Bedrock AgentCore (memory, tools, gateway, identity, policies, evaluators), the Model Context Protocol, OpenAPI and tool use, humans in the loop, Amazon Q Business and Q Apps, and Amazon Quick (Spaces, connectors, Flows, Automate, Research and Apps).

Operational efficiency and optimisation — token efficiency, cost-effective model selection, resource utilisation and throughput, intelligent caching, building responsive systems, optimising retrieval performance, foundation model system performance, exponential backoff and connection pooling, and Amazon Bedrock cross-region inference.

The five things I'll actually remember

1. An agent runs a loop, and the model isn't doing the work. Think, pick a tool, act, check, repeat. The model decides what to do next — something else does each job. That made agents far less mysterious.

2. The tool list is your real control. An agent can only ever do what you gave it tools for. And a tool's plain-English description isn't documentation — it's the thing that makes the agent choose correctly.

3. Ask what happens if it's wrong, not whether it can. Almost always it can. Looking up an order — let it run. Refunding a large amount or deleting a record — put a person in front of it.

4. Get identity right early. It's easy to build an agent with admin permissions, because that's the version that works first time. But then anyone talking to it can reach anything it can reach. The agent should act as the person asking.

5. A prompt is a running cost, not a one-off. You pay per token, in and out, on every single request. Retrieve three pages instead of twenty. Summarise old chat history. Ask for shorter answers. And cache the stable questions — never the personal ones.

One more, on the difference between a demo and a product: a chatbot that gives a bad answer is embarrassing. An agent that gets it wrong has done something. The mistake isn't a sentence, it's an event — which is why policies and evaluators matter more here than they did last block.

What I'd do differently

Check what AWS is deprecating before following a tutorial. Bedrock Agents and Q Business are both being retired, and plenty of guides still teach them. Five minutes checking the current service page would have saved me starting in the wrong place.

